{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1389552-20th-anniversary-botches-specialties","id":1389552,"title":"20th Anniversary Botches & Specialties","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-05-17T20:30:48-07:00","id":1389552,"title":"20th Anniversary Botches & Specialties","content":"I saw another thread about &quot;how do you run specialties in your games?&quot; But I am curious, specifically, about how folks handle the interaction between &quot;rolling a 1&quot; and having a specialty, in their own games? This post assumes specialties are run as written in the W20 Core Rulebook (a die showing a 10, with a relevant specialty, &quot;counts as two successes&quot;).<br /> <br /> Tonight in my session, I found that two of my players were operating under one interpretation, and others at the table were operating under another.<br /> <br /> I have always read the rules as defining a &quot;success&quot; as a die that shows a number equal to, or greater than, the difficulty of the task - and that a die showing a 1 would each remove one of these &quot;successes&quot; from the dice roll, before you calculated the total.<br /> <br /> I have also read that specialties mean that a rolled 10 results in that success counting as two successes.<br /> <br /> Perhaps a literal interpretation, but because the book goes out of its way to describe this process as setting the dice aside, it always read to me that literally.<br /> <br /> A couple players forwarded to me, tonight, that they have been treating it like a rolled 10, in a specialty, &quot;adds two successes to their pool&quot; and thus only one of those is lost to a rolled 1. My understanding would be that the 1 would remove &quot;a success&quot; (defined as above, vis-a-vis the *die*, not its value), so if the success removed was a 10 you would lose both of the successes that it was worth.<br /> <br /> <i>An analogy, in case it is needed, for clarity:<br /> One definition asserts that every die that rolls equal to, or greater than, the difficulty places a $1 bill on the table. If you have a specialty, and roll a 10, two $1 bills are placed on the table. Then each 1 rolled removes a bill from the table, and your number of successes left over would be equal to the number of $ you have left. In this instance, rolling a 10 with a specialty could insulate you against two 1's rolled.<br /> <br /> One definition asserts that every die that rolls equal to, or greater than, the difficulty places a $1 bill on the table. If you have a speciality, and roll a 10, a single $2 bill is placed on the table, instead. Then each 1 rolled removes a bill from the table, and your number of successes left over would be equal to the number of $ you have left. In this instance, rolling a 1 could cost you both successes from a rolled 10, if you don't have another bill to sacrifice to the 1.</i><br /> <br /> This has never been a question, in my mind - as a &quot;success&quot; was the name given to a die that had the requisite number showing, and a speciality clearly made dice showing a 10 &quot;worth two.&quot; I never conceived of it as otherwise, in part because of how specifically the book references the physical dice when describing the methodology. Upon searching things, I am seeing that a lot of folk use the &quot;exploding tens&quot; method that is described in other White Wolf supplements (and in older editions of Werewolf), some even acting like W20 *says* that same thing... which my physical copy *clearly* doesn't.<br /> <br /> So, after a spirited conversation with these players, tonight, I wanted to put this specific question to the community - how do you handle the interaction between Specialties and the &quot;rule of 1/botches/etc.&quot; rules?<br /> <br /> I do want to be clear, I am not so much looking for a debate on how the rules for Specialties should be written (such as going back to exploding 10s, etc.) unless it is specifically relevant to your experiences governing the rules as written. I have some strong opinions of my own on how specialties should work differently than they do - I just want to make sure I have a clear, fair, and thorough understanding of how *these rules, as written* should interact with each other.<br /> <br /> Thanks for any insight!<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25675-anna-fianna","name":"Anna Fianna","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25675_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}